@startuml PassTheKeysDefended
title Defended - passkey-bound DPoP: sender-constrained sessions, key held by the authenticator

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName sans-serif
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam ParticipantPadding 14
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "Victim" as V
box "The victim's device" #E7F6EC
  participant "Authenticator\n(passkey, holds the private key)" as AUTH
  participant "Victim's tab (/login)\n+ PasskeyProvider" as VP
end box
participant "Relying party\nverifies + binds the session" as RP
box "Attacker's site (separate origin)" #FDECEC
  participant "Attacker's tab" as AP
  participant "Back-end\ndead-drop + proxy" as AD
end box
actor "Attacker" as A

note across
  This is DPoP (sender-constrained, proof-of-possession per request) - with the PoP key held by
  the authenticator instead of minted in the page. The session token is bound to a key
  thumbprint (**jkt**), and every request carries a fresh proof over method + URL + nonce. The
  only twist vs RFC 9449: the key is the passkey credential (private key never leaves the
  authenticator) and the proof is a WebAuthn assertion, so a compromised page cannot choose,
  read, or relay the key.
end note

== Attacker starts a login, but will not finish it ==
A -> AP : open the attacker site
AP -> RP : start a passkey login (via proxy)
RP --> AP : nonce **Ca**
AP -> AD : publish **Ca** in the dead-drop

== Victim's page is compromised and relays the sign-in ==
V -> VP : click "Sign in"
VP -> AD : fetch the nonce (cross-origin) -> **Ca**
VP -> AUTH : get(**Ca**)  (user verifies)
AUTH --> VP : assertion over **Ca**, signed by the passkey\n(the private key stays in the authenticator)
VP -> AD : hand over the assertion\n(there is no page-held key to leak)

== Attacker finishes the login - and is stuck ==
AP -> AD : take the assertion
AP -> RP : finish the login with it
RP -> RP : verify the signature; DPoP-bind the session\ntoken to the credential's key thumbprint (**jkt**)
RP --> AP : a real session token for the victim  (DPoP-bound to the passkey)
AP -> RP : GET /api/me  (Authorization: Passkey <token>)
note right of AP : the DPoP proof is a FRESH assertion from the\nvictim's authenticator; this machine has none
RP --> AP : **401** - missing passkey proof (no DPoP proof)
AP -> A : "Session captured, but locked"

note across #E7F6EC
  Same guarantee as DPoP - a stolen/relayed token is useless without the bound key - but the
  DPoP key is in the authenticator, below the page. Finishing the ceremony is not enough: every
  resource call needs a fresh DPoP proof signed by the passkey, the attacker's machine has no
  authenticator, and the key never leaves the victim's device. A compromised page can coax at
  most one more user-approved proof out of the authenticator - it can never hand over the key,
  so there is no durable, relayable session.
end note

@enduml
